# Line-ending / byte-fidelity policy for cpp-kana2.
#
# Why this file exists: conformance/cases.sha256 is checked against conformance/cases.json's BYTES.
# On Windows git's default core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF as CRLF on checkout, so a fixture generated
# with LF would hash one way on the machine that generated it and another way in a fresh clone.
# `text eol=lf` is the narrow fix: git stores LF and every checkout gets LF back on every platform.
# There is deliberately no blanket `*` rule -- that would change how every existing file is stored.

conformance/cases.json text eol=lf
conformance/cases.sha256 text eol=lf

# The mora table and the corpus under tests/data are DATA: git must not normalise them at all, so the
# bytes a reader sees (and that check_data_tables.py hashes) are the bytes committed.
tests/data/** -text
data/** -text

# Every script the suites run through `bash` / every source file: LF everywhere. The second reason for
# sources is the clang-format the tree was formatted with; on a
# CRLF checkout that output differs by every line ending and the gate would fail on a machine where
# nothing is actually wrong.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.c text eol=lf
*.cc text eol=lf
*.cpp text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
*.hpp text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.cs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.inc text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf
*.cmake text eol=lf


